    <body>
      <sec id="article-18151.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Bartonellosis encompasses a group of emerging zoonotic infections caused by <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> species, fastidious, gram-negative, facultative, intracellular bacteria that infect red blood cells and endothelial cells, often causing prolonged bacteremia in humans and animals. Over 45 <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> species have been identified, with 12 linked to human disease. Clinically significant species include <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella henselae</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella quintana</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella bacilliformis</italic>. <italic toggle="yes">B henselae</italic> causes cat-scratch disease, culture-negative endocarditis, and bacillary peliosis hepatis. <italic toggle="yes">B quintana</italic>, historically associated with trench fever, also causes chronic bacteremia, bacillary angiomatosis, and subacute endocarditis, particularly in individuals living in crowded or unhygienic conditions. <italic toggle="yes">B bacilliformis</italic>, endemic to South America, causes Carrion disease, presenting as acute Oroya fever with high mortality if untreated, followed by verruga peruana. Bartonellosis is often underrecognized due to nonspecific clinical presentations.</p>
        <p>This course equips participants to recognize bartonellosis, interpret clinical and laboratory findings, and apply species-specific antimicrobial strategies. Clinicians identify at-risk individuals and initiate evaluation, microbiologists confirm diagnoses, pharmacists guide antimicrobial selection, and nurses monitor adherence and response. Interprofessional collaboration is highlighted, which enhances early detection, optimized management, and improved survival and quality of life for patients with this disease.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Create differential diagnoses for the most common species of <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> that cause infection: <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella henselae, Bartonella quintana </italic>and<italic toggle="yes"> Bartonella bacilliformis</italic></p></list-item><list-item><p>Interpret the current evidence-based guidelines to correctly diagnose bartonellosis in patients with suspected disease.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based guidelines to select the appropriate antimicrobial regimen to treat patients with bartonellosis.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement effective collaboration and communication among interprofessional team members to improve outcomes and treatment efficacy for patients with bartonellosis.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=18151&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=18151">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18151.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic>&#x000a0;spp are intracellular, facultative, gram-negative bacteria that&#x000a0;cause zoonotic infections in humans globally.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> is an emerging and expanding genus, currently reported to have over 45 species, 12 of which are associated with human infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r1">[1]</xref>. The species most commonly associated with bartonellosis in humans are<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;B henselae, B quintana&#x000a0;</italic>and<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;B bacilliformis</italic><italic toggle="yes">. B henselae </italic>causes&#x000a0;cat-scratch disease, and other clinical syndromes, such as culture-negative endocarditis and bacillary peliosis hepatis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B quintana</italic>, a louse-borne infection that was historically the cause of trench fever, is a neglected cause of syndromes, such as acute febrile illness, chronic bacteremia, bacillary angiomatosis, and subacute endocarditis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Transmission of <italic toggle="yes">B quintana</italic> is by infected body lice, and risk factors include&#x000a0;living in crowded, unhygienic conditions&#x000a0;as well as&#x000a0;experiencing homelessness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Recent reports have documented transmission of <italic toggle="yes">B quintana</italic> through organ transplantation from deceased persons who experienced homelessness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B bacilliformis</italic>&#x000a0;is endemic to the South American Andean valleys and causes Carrion disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r8">[8]</xref>
<italic toggle="yes">B</italic>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">bacilliformis</italic> is transmitted to humans&#x000a0;via the bite of the sandfly <italic toggle="yes">Lutzomyia&#x000a0;verrucarum. </italic></p>
        <p>Carrion disease&#x000a0;presents in 2 clinical forms:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The acute phase&#x000a0;of&#x000a0;Oroya fever&#x000a0;is a severe, febrile, hemolytic illness with untreated mortality rates&#x000a0;reaching up to 80% to 90%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r8">[8]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The chronic phase,&#x000a0;known as verruga peruana, typically appears weeks to months later as indolent and disfiguring angioproliferative skin lesions, which are&#x000a0;rarely life-threatening.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Unlike other <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> species, <italic toggle="yes">B bacilliformis</italic> is exclusively linked to these 2 manifestations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;This review focuses on human infections&#x000a0;caused by&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B</italic><italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;henselae, B quintana, and B bacilliformis,</italic>&#x000a0;the most common causes of emerging bartonellosis in humans, with significant epidemiological implications.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18151.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Bartonellae</italic> are considered an emerging and expanding genus, with more than 45 <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella&#x000a0;</italic>species currently described, at least 12 of which have been associated with human infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r1">[1]</xref>
<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> spp are gram-negative, facultative intracellular bacteria transmitted to humans through arthropod vectors or direct contact with animals.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B henselae</italic>&#x000a0;is responsible for cat-scratch disease, a zoonotic disease typically presenting with regional lymphadenitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B quintana</italic>, historically recognized as the agent of trench fever, is transmitted by the human body louse and may also manifest as endocarditis or bacillary angiomatosis, particularly in immunocompromised hosts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;In contrast, <italic toggle="yes">B bacilliformis</italic>, restricted to Andean regions and transmitted by sand flies, produces Carrion disease, characterized by a biphasic course, with acute Oroya fever followed by a chronic angioproliferative cutaneous eruption known as verruga peruana.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18151.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic>&#x000a0;spp are intracellular, facultative, gram-negative, slow-growing bacteria that&#x000a0;cause zoonotic infections in humans globally. Bartonella is an emerging and expanding genus, currently comprising over 45 different species, 12 of which are associated with human diseases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;These Bartonella species include:&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B henselae,&#x000a0;B quintana,&#x000a0;B bacilliformis,&#x000a0;B elizabethae,&#x000a0;B grahamii,&#x000a0;B clarridgeiae,&#x000a0;B koehlerae,&#x000a0;B vinsonii (</italic>especially subspecies <italic toggle="yes">B&#x000a0;berkhoffii </italic>and<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;B arupensis),&#x000a0;B washoensis, B rochalimae, B melophagi, B alsatica,&#x000a0;</italic>and<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;B tamiae (</italic>see <bold>Table 1</bold>&#x000a0;below).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> exemplifies the advantages of integrated approaches: <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> spp infect various animal hosts, are increasingly recognized as causative agents of human disease, and are transmitted by arthropod vectors, such as fleas, lice, and sand flies. Bartonellosis&#x000a0;is characterized by long-lasting erythrocyte-associated bacteremia across multiple reservoir hosts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;Advances in molecular epidemiology have highlighted the extensive diversity within the genus <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic>. Over time, multiple species have been identified, each exhibiting host specificity to different mammalian reservoirs and relying on distinct arthropod vectors for transmission.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r11">[11]</xref> These infections appear globally distributed and affect a wide range of species. At least 13 <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> species are currently considered pathogenic to humans, with <italic toggle="yes">B bacilliformis</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">B quintana</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">B henselae</italic> accounting for most clinically significant cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> infections are often persistent and asymptomatic in their natural reservoir hosts. These bacteria can infect various cell types, including erythrocytes, endothelial cells, human stem cells, and macrophages.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Erythrocyte invasion is specific to the host and is mediated by the Trw type IV secretion system, which promotes selective adhesion to erythrocytes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r12">[12]</xref> Both reservoir and incidental hosts can develop localized tissue lesions, and bacterial replication within vascular tissues may lead to angioproliferative growths and inflammation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Evidence suggests that ticks, red ants, and spiders&#x000a0;may transmit&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r14">[14]</xref> Bed bugs have been implicated in the transmission cycle of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B quintana</italic>&#x000a0;and have been artificially infected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r15">[15]</xref> The diversity of newly discovered&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic>&#x000a0;species, the large number and ecologically diverse animal reservoir hosts, and the large spectrum of arthropod vectors that can transmit these bacteria among animals and humans are significant causes for public health concern.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-18151.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 1. Bartonella spp: Main Clinical Syndrome, Vector, and Reservoir</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="2" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
