            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">
<bold>B henselae</bold>
</italic>
</p>
        <p>Cats and the cat flea, <italic toggle="yes">Ctenocephalides felis,</italic> act as vectors for transmission of cat scratch disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Study results have confirmed&#x000a0;that the flea transmits&#x000a0;the pathogen, which is critical for sustaining the circulation of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B henselae</italic>&#x000a0;within the feline population. Transmission occurs when flea feces containing the bacteria contaminate feeding sites, scratches, or bite wounds, enabling spread between cats and from cats to humans.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r17">[17]</xref> Within the flea, <italic toggle="yes">B henselae</italic> replicates in the intestinal tract and is continuously shed in the feces over the insect&#x02019;s approximately 12-day lifespan&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Many cats harbor <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> infections without exhibiting overt clinical symptoms, although they may undergo intermittent bacteremia that lasts for prolonged periods. Concurrent infection with multiple <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> species is frequently observed. More recent research has shown that other arthropod vectors, including ticks, lice, chiggers, and mosquitoes, are likely also implicated in the spread of the disease among humans.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> spp infection in cats has been reported as endemic in many countries, and studies in humans have found variable seroprevalence rates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r19">[19]</xref> The global incidence of cat-scratch disease was reported to be 6.4 cases per 100,000 in adults and 9.4 cases per 100,000 in children aged 5 to 9 years, respectively. The highest incidence is reported in the southeastern United States, peaking in January, late summer, and fall.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">
<bold>B quintana</bold>
</italic>
</p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">B quintan</italic>a is a bacterium restricted to human hosts and louse vectors. Epidemic and sporadic outbreaks of trench fever have been reported globally since the early 20th century, with the most significant epidemics occurring during World Wars I and II.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Trench fever, the earliest recognized disease caused by <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella quintana</italic>, is estimated to have affected more than one million individuals during World War I.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;The human body louse, <italic toggle="yes">Pediculus humanus,</italic>&#x000a0;is the predominant vector responsible for the transmission of trench fever caused by B<italic toggle="yes">artonella quintana</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Bartonellosis with&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B quintana</italic>&#x000a0;is now a reemerging infection, transmitted by the human body louse in conditions of poverty, overcrowding, and in persons who experience homelessness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;Transmission of <italic toggle="yes">B quintana</italic> has also been reported through organ transplantation&#x000a0;from harvested organs, mostly from high-risk populations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Clinical manifestations include trench fever, culture-negative endocarditis, chronic bacteremia, and bacillary angiomatosis, a&#x000a0;proliferative vascular disease seen in immunocompetent, but primarily immunocompromised patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">
<bold>B bacilliformis</bold>
</italic>
</p>
        <p>Carrion disease is&#x000a0;caused by <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella bacilliformis, w</italic>hich is&#x000a0;endemic to Peru, Colombia, and Ecuador; sporadic cases have been reported in Chile, Guatemala, and Bolivia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;The transmission of <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella bacilliformis</italic> occurs&#x000a0;via the bite of the female sandfly <italic toggle="yes">Lutzomyia.&#x000a0;</italic>Infections with&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B. bacilliformis are</italic>&#x000a0;characterized by two distinct clinical forms: Oroya fever, an acute febrile bacteremia&#x000a0;characterized by fever and hemolytic anemia, which carries a mortality rate up to 90% if left untreated. Verruga Peruana is a chronic, eruptive cutaneous disease that can emerge weeks and months after the acute phase.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18151.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Once inside the host, <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic>&#x000a0;spp primarily target CD34+ progenitor cells, which are precursors of endothelial cells that line blood vessels and tissues. Following entry, the bacterium inhibits programmed cell death and establishes a protective vacuole that shelters it from host defenses.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r22">[22]</xref> A key virulence factor is lipid A, an endotoxin present in the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r22">[22]</xref> The capacity of <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> to invade both erythrocytes and endothelial cells is central to its pathogenesis. By modulating immune responses, the pathogen increases host vulnerability to secondary bacterial infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r22">[22]</xref> After the initial acute phase, <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> disseminates into the endovascular, lymphatic, and other organ systems, resulting in a wide range of clinical manifestations.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18151.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>A comprehensive personal, travel, and social history should be obtained, and a physical examination should be performed in any patient seeking medical attention. In cases of suspected cat scratch disease, the clinical history may be vague or poorly reported, as the initial inoculation is often unnoticed. The differential diagnosis for each syndrome can be extensive, and diagnosis may be delayed due to the need for a comprehensive workup and the fact that <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> infection is often not initially considered.</p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">
<bold>Bartonella henselae</bold>
</italic>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cat scratch disease (CSD)&#x000a0;</bold>is transmitted by a bite, lick, or scratch of open skin by an infected cat. Cutaneous manifestations typically begin within 1 to 10 days of exposure and progress through vesicular, erythematous, and papular phases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Regional lymphadenopathy typically appears 1 to 3 weeks after inoculation; fever is often reported. CSD is usually benign and self-limiting. Disseminated CSD with granulomatous involvement&#x000a0;of visceral organs, eyes, and bones can occur, but this is rare.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Infective neuroretinitis</bold>: After the lymphatic system, the eye is the most frequently affected organ; 5% to 10% of CSD cases present with ocular manifestations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;Ocular involvement can be subclinical, and visual symptoms may not be a common presenting complaint. Neuroretinitis is an optic neuropathy classically characterized by optic disc swelling in the presence of a partial or complete macular star.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Parinaud oculo-glandular syndrom</bold>e, characterized by tender lymphadenopathy of the pre-auricular, submandibular, and cervical lymph nodes, often co-occurs with conjunctival, eyelid, and adjacent skin surface infections, affecting approximately 2 to 8% of patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r28">[28]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neurological manifestations</bold>&#x000a0;reported in patients diagnosed with cat scratch disease include encephalopathy, transverse myelitis, radiculitis, and cerebellar ataxia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r29">[29]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Culture-negative endocarditis.&#x000a0;</bold>Another presentation of infection includes negative culture endocarditis. Although several species have been implicated in causing endocarditis, <italic toggle="yes">B henselae</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella quintana</italic> account for approximately 95% of endocarditis cases caused by <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r30">[30]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Severe, progressive, disseminated disease</bold> may occur in immunocompromised individuals, especially those with HIV infection and organ transplants. Without therapy, the infection spreads systemically and can involve virtually any organ, and the outcome is sometimes fatal.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Bacillary angiomatosis</bold>&#x000a0;is a vascular proliferative disorder that affects immunocompromised individuals, especially in those with HIV/AIDS.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;BA is caused by either <italic toggle="yes">B henselae </italic>or<italic toggle="yes"> B quintana&#x000a0;</italic>and is&#x000a0;characterized by cutaneous lesions, subcutaneous masses, spleen, liver, and bone lesions. This is a vascular proliferative disease that most often involves the skin, but may also affect other organs. The clinical differential diagnosis includes pyogenic granuloma, hemangioma, subcutaneous tumors, and Kaposi sarcoma. The skin lesions are very similar to those reported for verruga peruana, the chronic form of Carrion's disease. Lesions can also involve the bone marrow, liver, spleen, or lymph nodes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Peliosis hepatis (PH)</bold> is&#x000a0;a vascular proliferation of sinusoidal hepatic capillaries resulting in blood-filled spaces in the liver.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r2">[2]</xref> This disease was first described in patients with tuberculosis and advanced cancers and correlated with the use of drugs such as anabolic steroids.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B henselae</italic> is now recognized as an infectious cause of PH in HIV-infected patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;PH has also been reported in organ transplant recipients and can occur simultaneously with splenic peliosis and bacillary angiomatosis of the skin in HIV-infected individuals.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">
<bold>Bartonella quintana</bold>
</italic>
</p>
        <p>Trench fever, caused by <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella quintana,</italic> presents clinical features including malaise, fever, headache, bone pain, and splenomegaly. The pattern of fever associated with trench fever is highly variable, ranging from a single episode to a persistent fever lasting several days.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">
<bold>Bartonella bacilliformis</bold>
</italic>
</p>
        <p>The clinical manifestations of infection caused by <italic toggle="yes">B bacilliformis</italic> include an acute phase, Oroya fever, characterized by fever, malaise, headache, intraerythrocytic anemia, tachycardia, and hepatomegaly, and a chronic phase (verruga peruana), characterized by endothelial cell-derived, blood-filled tumors erupting cutaneously, which appear approximately 2 to 8 weeks after the acute phase.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;Chronic <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> infection can be associated with relapsing low-grade fever.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r35">[35]</xref> Chronic eye problems include blurred vision, photophobia, and eye irritation.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> commonly infects the bone marrow, resulting in bone pain, most commonly in the tibia. Anemia can occur from <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> scavenging nutrients from red blood cells. Neurological symptoms include poor balance, decreased cognition, memory impairment, insomnia, and restlessness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r35">[35]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18151.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The diagnosis of CSD requires a multifaceted approach that integrates clinical presentation with molecular, serological, and histopathological findings. The diagnostic modalities commonly used to diagnose&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> infection include: serological testing, culture, histopathology, and polymerase chain reaction (PCR).&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Serology&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Serology remains the&#x000a0;most cost-effective&#x000a0;initial diagnostic test. Indirect fluorescent antibody (IFA) or enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays can detect antibodies against&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B henselae</italic>. IFA was the first microbiological test available for CSD; however, its positive predictive value is variable, especially during the early stages of the disease. In some studies' results, the positive predictive value of IFA reached 91.4%, whereas other results reported lower sensitivity. Typical sensitivity of Bartonella henselae immunoglobulin (Ig) G and M serology ranges from approximately 50% to 80%, with specificity generally high at 90% to 100%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r36">[36]</xref>&#x000a0;Sensitivity increases when clinical suspicion for CSD is high. An IgM titer of 1:16 or greater indicates an acute infection, which is detectable for up to 3 months in approximately 50% of patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r37">[37]</xref> An IgG titer of 1:256 or greater is evidence of current or past infection. Unlike culture and PCR, serology does not rely on the presence of bacteria in the blood and can also be used to monitor response to therapy, even though titers may persist for years. A drawback of serological testing is that cross-reactions occur between antibodies for&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B henselae</italic>&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B quintana</italic>&#x000a0;and with both&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Chlamydia</italic>&#x000a0;species and <italic toggle="yes">Coxiella burnetii,&#x000a0;</italic>which may&#x000a0;limit interpretation. Furthermore, serological tests do not differentiate&#x000a0;between <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic>&#x000a0;species.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Culture</bold>
</p>
        <p>The culture of <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> species provides a definitive diagnosis, but it is time-consuming, expensive, and technically challenging.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> spp&#x000a0;are slow-growing and fastidious, with primary isolation often requiring 1 to 4 weeks of incubation on blood agar plates and, in some cases, up to 21 days.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r38">[38]</xref> Due to these limitations, culture is not routinely recommended in clinical practice.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Visualization by special stains.</bold>
</p>
        <p>Histopathological examination of the affected tissue typically reveals neutrophilic and lymphocytic infiltration, along with scattered cellular debris. <bold>Warthin-Starry silver staining</bold> can reveal small, darkly staining bacteria, while electron microscopy demonstrates pleomorphic bacilli with a trilaminar cell wall. For <italic toggle="yes">B bacilliformis</italic>, <bold>Giemsa staining</bold> highlights cytoplasmic inclusions known as Rocha-Lima inclusions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Polymerase chain reaction</bold>
</p>
        <p>PCR assays can be used to detect DNA from tissue samples, such as lymph nodes or other clinical specimens,&#x000a0;eg, blood, to detect&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic>&#x000a0;DNA.&#x000a0;PCR offers high specificity and rapid identification; however, its sensitivity varies, ranging from 30% to 76% depending on the sample type, species, and disease stage. When tested on blood and culture samples, a study by Choat et al reported a real-time PCR test targeting the vomp region of B quintana with 100% sensitivity and specificity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r40">[40]</xref> &#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18151.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> species demonstrate susceptibility to numerous antibiotics when grown in culture, including penicillins, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides, chloramphenicol, tetracyclines, macrolides, rifampin, fluoroquinolones, and co-trimoxazole. Nonetheless, in vitro activity does not reliably predict in vivo effectiveness. For instance, despite exhibiting a very low minimum inhibitory concentration&#x000a0;in vitro, penicillin has proven ineffective in clinical settings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>Management of cat scratch disease depends on the clinical presentation of the disease. Patients presenting with mild symptoms, such as lymphadenopathy and fever, should be offered supportive care, as the disease is usually self-limiting and antimicrobial therapy is not necessary.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;However, study results have shown that using a single antimicrobial regimen will significantly reduce the duration and size of the lymphadenopathy. The antimicrobial agents that can be used include erythromycin, doxycycline, azithromycin, ciprofloxacin, gentamicin, and rifampin.</p>
        <p>Depending on the disease and pathogen, these agents can be used in various combinations and for different durations of therapy:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><italic toggle="yes"><bold>B henselae</bold></italic>&#x000a0;for patients with CSD and extensive lymphadenopathy
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Erythromycin, doxycycline, or oral azithromycin at 500 mg on the first day and 250 mg on days 2 to 5</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><italic toggle="yes"><bold>B henselae</bold></italic>&#x000a0;for patients with CSD neuroretinitis
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Oral doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 4&#x000a0;to 6 weeks and oral rifampin 300 mg twice daily for 4&#x000a0;to 6 weeks</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><italic toggle="yes"><bold>B quintana</bold></italic>&#x000a0;for trench fever or chronic bacteremia
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Oral doxycycline at 200 mg once daily for 4 weeks&#x000a0;plus intravenous gentamicin 3 mg/kg for 2 weeks&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r43">[43]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><italic toggle="yes"><bold>B quintana </bold></italic>for&#x000a0;bacillary angiomatosis and peliosis hepatis
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Treat with oral erythromycin 500 mg or doxycycline 4 times daily for 3 to 4 months</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold><italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> culture-positive endocarditis</bold> should be treated with a multidisciplinary approach with antibiotics
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Oral doxycycline 100 mg for 6 weeks, plus&#x000a0;intravenous gentamicin&#x000a0;plus rifampin for 14 days, and surgery when necessary&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r44">[44]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r45">[45]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neuroretinitis and CNS involvement:&#x000a0;</bold>There is no strong evidence to support the treatment<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>A combination of oral doxycycline 100 mg twice daily and rifampin 300 mg twice daily has been suggested for complicated cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Oroya fever </bold>management typically&#x000a0;includes antibiotics such as tetracyclines, aminoglycosides, and macrolides.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Combination therapy with more than 1 agent&#x000a0;may be recommended to improve efficacy and reduce the risk of resistance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r41">[41]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18151.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of bartonellosis&#x000a0;depends on the species of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic>&#x000a0;and the clinical syndrome it causes. The 3 most common species that cause infections are <italic toggle="yes">B henselae</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">B quintana</italic>,<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;B bacilliformis.</italic>&#x000a0;Their presentations are different:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold><italic toggle="yes">B henselae</italic></bold>&#x000a0;can cause CSD, which most commonly presents as lymphadenitis.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p><bold>The differential diagnosis of CSD:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Mycobacterial infections (tuberculous or atypical mycobacteria)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Toxoplasmosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Viral lymphadenitis (EBV, CMV, HIV)</p></list-item><list-item><p><italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Streptococcus pyogenes lymphadenitis</italic></p></list-item><list-item><p>Tularemia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Lymphoma or leukemia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coccidioidomycosis and valley fever</p></list-item><list-item><p>Leishmaniosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Nocardiosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Sarcoidosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Sporotrichosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Syphilis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Toxoplasmosis</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold><italic toggle="yes">B quintana&#x000a0;</italic></bold>can manifest as trench fever, relapsing bacteremia, endocarditis, or bacillary angiomatosis.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p><bold>Differential diagnosis of trench fever and relapsing bacteremia:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Other relapsing fevers (Borrelia recurrentis,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Borrelia miyamotoi</italic>)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Malaria (<italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium</italic>&#x000a0;spp)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Typhus group rickettsioses (<italic toggle="yes">Rickettsia prowazekii</italic>,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Rickettsia typhi</italic>)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Brucellosis (<italic toggle="yes">Brucella</italic>&#x000a0;spp)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Typhoid fever (<italic toggle="yes">Salmonella enterica</italic>&#x000a0;serovar Typhi)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Viral infections (Epstein-Barr virus, cytomegalovirus, HIV acute retroviral syndrome, influenza, adenovirus)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Q fever (<italic toggle="yes">Coxiella burnetii</italic>)</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Differential diagnosis of culture-negative endocarditis</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p><italic toggle="yes">Coxiella burnetii&#x000a0;</italic>(Q fever endocarditis)</p></list-item><list-item><p><italic toggle="yes">Brucella</italic></p></list-item><list-item><p><italic toggle="yes">Tropheryma whipplei</italic></p></list-item><list-item><p>Noninfectious: Libman-Sacks endocarditis,&#x000a0;marantic endocarditis&#x000a0;(malignancy, hypercoagulable states)</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold><italic toggle="yes">B bacilliformis</italic>
</bold>causes Acute Oroya Fever and Verruga Peruana.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p><bold>Acute Oroya Fever differential diagnosis:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Autoimmune hemolytic anemia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency with oxidative stress</p></list-item><list-item><p>Dengue (severe form)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Yellow fever</p></list-item><list-item><p>Rickettsial infections<bold>&#x000a0;</bold></p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Chronic verruga veruana (eruptive/angioproliferative phase) differential diagnosis:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Kaposi sarcoma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bacillary angiomatosis (<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella henselae, Bartonella quintana</italic>)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Pyogenic granuloma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cutaneous leishmaniasis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Angiosarcoma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hemangiomas and vascular malformations</p></list-item><list-item><p>Warts (verruca vulgaris)</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18151.s10" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Prevention of <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> infections involves simple measures tailored to the specific mode of transmission:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cat scratch disease:</bold> Reducing transmission from cats can be achieved by providing proper care for pets, avoiding scratches or bites, keeping cats indoors whenever possible, regularly treating cats to eliminate fleas, and washing hands thoroughly after handling cats.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Trench fever:</bold> Preventing infection requires minimizing exposure to human body lice and promptly treating infestations if they occur.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Carrion disease (<italic toggle="yes">B bacilliformis</italic>):</bold> Prevention focuses on avoiding bites from infected sandflies. Protective measures include wearing clothing with long sleeves, using insect repellents, and sleeping under insecticide-treated bed nets in areas where&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B&#x000a0;</italic><italic toggle="yes">bacilliiformis</italic>&#x000a0;is endemic.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These preventive strategies, along with public education about vectors and risk factors, are crucial in reducing the incidence of <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic>-related diseases.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18151.s11" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>
<bold>Ongoing Research</bold>
</p>
        <p>Findings suggest that&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic>&#x000a0;spp can be used to induce angiogenesis, which could potentially be utilized to circumvent blocked arteries in the heart, promote the formation of new blood vessels in damaged limbs, and accelerate wound healing in damaged tissues.&#x000a0;Current investigations are focused on understanding the genetic basis of the mechanisms that are involved in the causation of angiogenesis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r48">[48]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18151.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>The diagnosis and management of CSD are best approached by an interprofessional team, including an infectious disease consultant, emergency department physician,&#x000a0;internal medicine physician and nursing staff. Management depends largely on the clinical presentation. Patients with mild disease, such as localized lymphadenopathy and low-grade fever, often do not require antimicrobial therapy due to the self-limiting nature of CSD.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-18151.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0; Supportive care, including pain management and monitoring, is typically sufficient for these cases.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-18151.s13">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
